 "cushionNotice": "Federal programs (Social Security, SSDI, SSI, SNAP) are the same in both places. The gaps open where **states** decide: the wage floor and whether a city may raise it, Medicaid, leave, loan ceilings, eviction rules, what counts as a felony. Stack them: a Memphis single parent of two earning $14 an hour meets a broken leg with no paid leave, no Medicaid, and a 279.5% loan as the fastest cash; most RJ families meet it with employer insurance, paid leave, savings and a lender who charges a fraction of that. That is the fence we can't see from inside.",
 "fences": [
  {
   "h": "Insulation",
   "text": "Our families' cushions — employer insurance, paid leave, a landlord who calls instead of filing, a Colorado wage floor — feel like normal life, not like decisions. So a Memphis family's fall looks like a choice."
  },
  {
   "h": "Physical",
   "text": "We sleep in Midtown, eat at well-known restaurants, and move by van from site to site. We see shelters and pantries — places people go *after* something went wrong — not the apartment complex, the courtroom or the factory fence line where the price was set. Memphis is also a destination city (Beale Street, Graceland, the pyramid); it is easy to be a tourist and a volunteer in the same afternoon without noticing the switch."
  },
  {
   "h": "Cognitive",
   "text": "What Denver students already know about Memphis usually comes from crime headlines, music history and the civil rights movement. Each is partial. The headlines erase the neighborhoods; the music erases the economics; the museum can make 1968 feel finished. We also arrive not knowing that Tennessee and Colorado have different laws about wages, health care and schools — so we read state decisions as local character."
  },
  {
   "h": "Affective",
   "text": "Fear (of neighborhoods we've only seen in the news), which can turn into avoidance or into a brave story to tell at home. Guilt, especially for white students in a majority-Black city, which can make a student perform or go silent. Pity at a shelter, which needs the guest to be sad. Satisfaction at a finished task — boxes sorted, apartment set up — that can stand in for understanding why it was needed. Keep three things apart: what you saw, what you were told and by whom, and what you concluded."
  },
  {
   "h": "Theological",
   "text": "We're so blessed — reading our comfort as God's favor. Treating service as charity *to* Memphis rather than as meeting a city whose churches built the civil rights movement and run much of its safety net now. Remembering that King came to Memphis for workers' wages and dignity — not for a day of service."
  },
  {
   "h": "Fear brought from Denver",
   "text": "— of the most dangerous city and of people without housing, learned from family, friends, news and Denver street corners."
  },
  {
   "h": "Race, felt but not placed",
   "text": "For some students this is the first time being among the only white people in a room. Name the discomfort honestly, then connect it to Memphis history and to who lives where in Denver."
  },
  {
   "h": "Helper and impact framing",
   "text": "— guests described as the people we helped, a mission to make Memphis better, staying late as proof of commitment. A finished task stands in for understanding."
  },
  {
   "h": "The group as refuge",
   "text": "A week like this can become mostly about your own group: bunkroom nights, cards, the talent show, van rides. Those are good. Ask more questions than feels comfortable, and take the nightly reflection seriously."
  },
  {
   "h": "Reluctance",
   "text": "— not the immersion a student wanted; not wanting to miss school."
  },
  {
   "h": "Comparison to home",
   "text": "— gated suburbs, a Denver boulevard, service hours done only to meet a graduation requirement. Useful when it turns into a question about Denver; a fence when it stays a contrast."
  },
  {
   "h": "Joy read as proof",
   "text": "— smiles at a dinner or a rec center taken to mean poverty is bearable."
  },
  {
   "h": "Letting a machine do the noticing",
   "text": "— pasting a chatbot's evaluation of whether you encountered people. The noticing is the part only you can do."
  }
 ],
